WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.820 --> 00:00:05.510
For a guy whose job description is literally
"crimefighter," Batman sure does spend an

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:07.950
awful lot of time fighting other heroes.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:13.290
You'd think having an entire asylum full of
homicidal clowns, living refrigerators, and

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:17.760
the occasional immortal bio`-terrorist would
keep him busy enough, but no.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.859
Every now and then some well-meaning do-gooder
gets in his way, and it's ON.

00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:25.810
Batman has a pretty strong track record against
his fellow heroes.

00:00:25.810 --> 00:00:29.890
Here are just a few who've felt the wrath
of the Bat.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:30.890
Superman

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:35.260
Historically speaking, Batman and Superman
have been best friends, and the World's Finest

00:00:35.260 --> 00:00:38.250
crimefighting team, since they met in the
mid-'40s.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.820
They share a mutual desire to devote their
lives to helping others, but like all friends,

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:44.740
they occasionally have their disagreements.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:49.870
We're willing to bet that most arguments between
pals don't usually devolve into one person

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:54.390
using a radioactive space rock to poison the
other, but these guys don't really seem like

00:00:54.390 --> 00:00:56.239
the calm and reasonable types.

00:00:56.239 --> 00:01:01.170
The two heroes most famously came to blows
at the climax of The Dark Knight Returns,

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:06.140
in which an aging, post-retirement Batman
enlists the help of Green Arrow, Robin, and

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.960
the entirety of Gotham City's power grid to
have a fistfight so brutal that he ended up

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.820
giving himself a heart attack.

00:01:12.820 --> 00:01:16.820
Their arguments aren't limited to non-canonical
alternate futures, either.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.000
Take a look at Hush, which is essentially
Batman's Greatest Hits wrapped around one

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.380
of comics' most ludicrously convoluted mysteries.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:29.640
We see Superman's mind taken over by Poison
Ivy, which leads Batman to put on a Kryptonite

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.750
ring and uppercut Superman back into his right
mind.

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:37.320
It's worth noting that both of those fights
involved Superman holding back, but hey: a

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:40.600
victory with an asterisk is still a victory.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.009
Green Lantern

00:01:42.009 --> 00:01:46.450
Then we have the highly controversial All
Star Batman and Robin the Boy Wonder.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:51.060
It's so controversial that you're probably
familiar with it even if you don't read comics.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.720
Batman cussing at Dick Grayson in the Batmobile?

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:56.970
Wonder Woman calling a random pedestrian "sperm
bank?"

00:01:56.970 --> 00:02:00.189
Batman and Black Canary getting turned on
by ultra-violence?

00:02:00.189 --> 00:02:04.680
It all happens in this one story, and you
can trust us: context doesn't really make

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:05.680
it better.

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:09.580
Batman's scene with Green Lantern, however,
is one of those rare moments where something

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:12.750
is so ridiculous that it loops back around
to being amazing.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:17.960
In order to face Hal Jordan, whose Green Lantern
ring can't affect anything yellow, Batman

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.410
lures him into a room where he has painted
literally everything yellow.

00:02:21.410 --> 00:02:22.460
The furniture.

00:02:22.460 --> 00:02:23.460
His costume.

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.020
He's even spray-painted his own face, and
to top it all off, he even taunts and sneers

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:32.800
at Green Lantern while sipping on a refreshing
glass of lemonade until he finally baits him

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.800
into a physical confrontation.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:39.020
Of course, it's actually Robin who does the
fighting, but considering that Batman's the

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:43.610
one who orchestrated the entire situation
so that a grown-ass superhero could get his

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:48.440
throat crushed by a ten year-old slathered
in spray paint, it's fair to say the win belongs

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:49.680
to him.

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:50.870
Guy Gardner

00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:53.080
Batman's Lantern problems are nothing new.

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:59.900
Coming at you from 1987's Justice League #5,
Batman landed the single most famous hero-vs.-hero

00:02:59.900 --> 00:03:04.310
punch in the history of the DC Universe, and
possibly all of comic history.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:09.250
On one side, you have Guy Gardner, the bowl-cut
Green Lantern commonly known as the biggest

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.750
jerk in the DC universe, constantly running
his mouth about how he should be running the

00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:14.750
team.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:15.910
On the other side, we have Batman.

00:03:15.910 --> 00:03:19.020
At this point, you probably know what happens
next.

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:23.940
The conflict builds for a few issues until
finally, Guy rears back to take a swing, and

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:28.370
Batman drops him with a single punch directly
to the nose, knocking him out cold.

00:03:28.370 --> 00:03:33.000
Guy is hit so hard that the punch crossed
realities into the Brave and the Bold animated

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:34.000
series.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:36.800
"You so much as sneeze without my permission
your are going to regret it."

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.220
"That I'd like to see"

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.810
"One punch, one punch!"

00:03:42.810 --> 00:03:44.000
And the best part?

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.120
Guy remains unconscious on the floor of the
Justice League's kitchen until halfway through

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:52.430
the next issue while the rest of the team
goes on a mission—then knocks himself back

00:03:52.430 --> 00:03:56.120
out by hitting his head on the underside of
a counter as soon as he wakes up.

00:03:56.120 --> 00:04:00.340
In other words, Batman punched him so hard
it knocked him out twice.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:01.340
Wildcat

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:02.930
It's a tale as old as time.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:07.440
Boxing champ Ted Grant was inspired by a Green
Lantern comic book and decided to dress up

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:11.840
as a six-foot kitty cat and punch criminals
until crime wasn't a thing anymore.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:12.840
Listen.

00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:14.590
It was 1941.

00:04:14.590 --> 00:04:16.530
Nobody really knew what they were doing back
then.

00:04:16.530 --> 00:04:20.820
Wildcat has traditionally been shown as one
of the fighters that trained Bruce Wayne in

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:25.780
the art of combat during his teenage years,
but in Brave and the Bold, the Joker was able

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.780
to force them to fight each other.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:28.780
How?

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:30.190
By threatening to shoot a puppy.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:32.870
Ultimately, they knocked each other out at
the same time.

00:04:32.870 --> 00:04:33.870
Listen.

00:04:33.870 --> 00:04:35.030
It was 1975.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:38.380
They definitely knew what they were doing,
but that doesn't mean they didn't get weird

00:04:38.380 --> 00:04:39.380
with it.

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:43.560
And if it's good enough for National Lampoon,
it's good enough for The Joker.

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:44.560
Punisher

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:48.630
While they're both on the same side in the
broadest possible way, the Punisher's tendency

00:04:48.630 --> 00:04:54.270
to leave his opponents as bullet-riddled corpses
clashes with Batman's time-tested code against

00:04:54.270 --> 00:04:58.460
killing, and that comes to a head when Batman
stops the Punisher from killing the Joker

00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:01.720
during Punisher/Batman: Deadly Knights.

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:06.270
Before Batman can explain that this is probably
why the Punisher doesn't have a lot of recurring

00:05:06.270 --> 00:05:11.400
villains, Frank Castle decks him with a hard
right, and Batman, in classic fashion, tells

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.650
him he let him have one punch because he knows
he's frustrated.

00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:19.470
When Punisher takes a second swing, however,
Batman doesn't just catch his fist, he casually

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:23.960
throws him face-first into a wall with a blunt
"I said one."

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:25.280
Captain America

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:29.860
Batman versus Captain America is one of the
most perfect matchups you could ask for.

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:33.680
The only problem is settling on a winner,
and while the odds would tend to favor the

00:05:33.680 --> 00:05:38.310
super-soldier over the world's greatest detective,
that's not always how it works out.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:43.810
In 1996's DC vs. Marvel, the decision was
left up to a vote by the fans.

00:05:43.810 --> 00:05:48.220
The result was a brawl that took place in
a sewer, in which the heroes fought each other

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:52.840
to a standstill, until a sudden rush of water
swamped Cap and gave Batman the chance to

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.030
bop him over the head with a batarang.

00:05:55.030 --> 00:05:59.780
It's not exactly the most dignified ending
to a fight, but hey, a victory with a technicality

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.250
is still a victory.

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:07.780
Interestingly enough, when the two characters
squared off again for 2003's JLA/Avengers,

00:06:07.780 --> 00:06:12.311
Batman conceded that while Captain America
could beat him in a straight-up fight, nobody

00:06:12.311 --> 00:06:13.530
had time for all that.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:14.990
"Ain't nobody got time for that!"

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:19.889
Which, when you think about it, is a pretty
great way to weasel your way out of a rematch.

00:06:19.889 --> 00:06:21.530
Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles

00:06:21.530 --> 00:06:25.900
The weirdest thing about the Batman/Teenage
Mutant Ninja Turtles crossover is that it

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.370
somehow didn't happen in 1990.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:33.560
It wasn't until 2015 that they actually met
up, but when they did, it was pretty much

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:39.270
everything you wanted to see: dimension-hopping
adventure, Shredder pitted against Ra's al-Ghul,

00:06:39.270 --> 00:06:42.389
and Mr. Freeze transformed into a mutant polar
bear.

00:06:42.389 --> 00:06:43.889
Which is pretty amazing.

00:06:43.889 --> 00:06:48.461
And of course, it also gives you a hero versus
hero fight, just to start things off, and

00:06:48.461 --> 00:06:51.980
unlike most of these, this one actually makes
a lot of sense.

00:06:51.980 --> 00:06:56.360
If you're Batman and you see a bunch of five-foot
turtles with ninja weapons running around

00:06:56.360 --> 00:07:00.431
your town, everything that's happened over
the past 75 years is going to lead you to

00:07:00.431 --> 00:07:03.300
the conclusion that they're probably up to
no good.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:07.680
Batman not only takes out each of the four
Ninja Turtles in turn and fights Splinter

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:12.730
to a draw before the Turtles have to retreat,
he also takes their weapons, and uses Raphael's

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:15.180
sai to shatter Leonardo's katana.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.800
In the end, the Turtles pretty much became
Batman's biggest fans.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:20.889
"Give me the camera"

00:07:20.889 --> 00:07:24.040
"Look, he's doing his Batman voice"

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.040
Hulk

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:30.330
It's the most improbable victory of all time,
and it happened in 1981's Batman vs. the Incredible

00:07:30.330 --> 00:07:31.330
Hulk.

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:35.120
For the most part, it's a pretty fantastic
fight, putting Batman on the defensive against

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:37.180
Hulk's unstoppable physical force.

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:42.370
It even includes awesome moments like Batman
busting out a Jackie Chan-style dodge by leaping

00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:46.830
straight through the open windows of a Hulk-hurled
car as it smashes against a wall.

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:51.050
The end of their first encounter, though,
has one of the biggest bits of belief-stretching

00:07:51.050 --> 00:07:54.270
since Captain America was swamped by sewer
water.

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:58.310
After realizing that punching isn't going
to get anywhere, Batman simply drops some

00:07:58.310 --> 00:08:03.020
knockout gas, and then delivers a kick to
the solar plexus so hard that the Hulk is

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:06.710
forced to take a deep inhale of that sweet,
sleepy cloud.

00:08:06.710 --> 00:08:10.400
Hulk hits the floor, and Batman celebrates
a victory that probably never should have

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:11.740
happened.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:17.420
Thanks for watching!

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:20.680
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:20.680 --> 00:08:23.120
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

